  {% set yesimPrimary = providerB.codes | findCode(providerB.primaryCode) %}

  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>Saily</strong> if you want the cheaper option and would normally pay for a VPN/ad-blocker separately when traveling anyway. Pick <strong>Yesim</strong> if you're a repeat traveler who wants a data balance that rolls over between trips and a real refund if you don't end up using it. Saily wins on price almost everywhere; Yesim's pitch is flexibility and a refund policy that goes further than most providers on this site.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: A Security Bundle or a Rollover Balance</h2>
  <p>
    Saily is NordVPN's own eSIM product (built in-house by Nord Security, not a licensing deal), so every plan ships with ad blocking, malware/phishing site blocking, and a virtual-location tool on top of the data connection. It also sells one universal eSIM that keeps working as you cross into a new supported country, instead of swapping profiles at each border.
  </p>
  <p>
    Yesim works differently: its "Pay & Fly" plans are a prepaid dollar balance, priced from $1.10/GB, rather than a fixed GB block, and whatever you don't use rolls over to your next trip instead of expiring. Yesim also claims automatic switching across 800+ partner networks ("SwitchLess Networks") so the eSIM hops to whichever local carrier has the best signal; that's Yesim's own claim, not something we've independently measured.
  </p>

