      <li><strong>Yesim:</strong> {{ providerB.primaryCode }}, {{ yesimPrimary.discount | lower }}. <a href="/yesim/">Full code list &rarr;</a></li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:.75rem;margin-bottom:0;color:#63553F;">Neither code is ours; both are the best public codes we've verified still work. Saily's applies storewide on every purchase. Yesim's is first-purchase only: returning customers should try WELCOMETOYESIM (15% off) or TRAVELFREE (a flat &euro;3 off) instead, both covered on the <a href="/yesim/">Yesim page</a>.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Is Saily or Yesim the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with Saily if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You want the lower price for comparable data, almost everywhere</li>
        <li>You'd normally pay for a VPN/ad-blocker separately when traveling anyway</li>
        <li>You're on a multi-country trip and don't want a new eSIM at every border</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with Yesim if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You travel often and want a balance that carries over instead of expiring per trip</li>
        <li>You might not use the whole plan and want a real refund option, not just a failed-activation refund</li>
        <li>You'd rather buy data in dollars than track a fixed GB allowance</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Saily can't be bought or topped up while you're physically inside Turkey.</strong> Its website and app were among those blocked by Turkey's telecom regulator (BTK) starting mid-2025. An eSIM purchased and installed before you land still works once you're there, so this only matters if you're planning to buy on arrival. We haven't found the same restriction reported for Yesim.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Yesim's refund guarantee is genuinely unusual for this category.</strong> Most eSIM providers on this site, Saily included, only refund a plan that never activated at all. Yesim advertises a 30-day money-back guarantee on plans you simply didn't end up using, which is the kind of flexibility that can offset its higher per-GB price if you tend to buy ahead of a trip and aren't sure you'll need the full plan.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>Saily vs Yesim FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, Saily or Yesim</h3>
      <p>Saily, in every destination we checked. Saily sits in our "Cheap" price bracket; Yesim sits in "Expensive." Yesim's rollover balance and refund guarantee are the trade-off for the higher price, not a cheaper rate per GB.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does either one include a VPN</h3>
      <p>Saily does: every plan bundles ad blocking, malware/phishing blocking, and a virtual-location tool, since Saily is built in-house by NordVPN's parent company. Yesim has no equivalent security bundle.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which one is better for a frequent traveler who buys data often</h3>
      <p>Yesim's Pay &amp; Fly balance rolls over between trips instead of expiring, which suits someone topping up the same balance repeatedly. Saily sells by the GB per plan instead, so unused data on one plan doesn't carry into the next purchase.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Can I get a refund if I don't use the plan</h3>
      <p>Yesim's 30-day money-back guarantee covers plans that were never used, not just ones that failed to activate. Saily's refund policy is narrower: a full refund if the eSIM was never installed or activated, or if less than 1% of the data was used, or there was a genuine service-quality issue.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

